          dealing: 'Cards shuffled; each player receives a stock of 26 cards face down. Traditionally the first cards are dealt two at a time, the remainder three at a time.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Get rid of all your cards by playing them to foundation piles (A-2-3-...-K in each suit) or onto your opponent's discard pile. The first to empty both stock and discard wins; the winner scores one point per card left in the opponent's stock and discard.",
          gameplay: "Non-dealer starts by turning the top card of her stock. If it is not an ace, she must discard it face up to start her discard pile and her turn ends. If it is an ace, she plays it to start that suit's foundation and continues turning stock cards, playing any that fit a foundation (ace or next higher in same suit), until one does not fit; that card is discarded and her turn ends. The dealer then plays: turn the top stock card; if it fits a foundation it must be played there; if it is one higher or one lower than the top of the opponent's discard (any suit) it may be played there. Continue turning stock and playing until an unplayable card is turned; discard it to your own pile and your turn ends. From each player's second turn onward, at any point you may also play the top card of your own discard pile to a foundation (mandatory if possible) or to the opponent's discard (optional). When you cannot or do not wish to play from discard, turn the next stock card and play as above. When stock is empty, turn your discard pile face down as a new stock. If you empty both stock and discard you win.",
          keyRules: [
            'Foundation piles build A-2-3-...-K in each suit in the center.',
            "You may play onto the opponent's discard if your card is exactly one rank higher or lower (suit irrelevant).",
            "If a player breaks the rules, opponent may call 'stop' and either force the correct play or end the offender's turn.",
            "Missing a play to the opponent's pile is legal; once you discard to your own pile or turn a stock card, you cannot undo it.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Follow the rules: play to foundations when possible, and consider when to play onto the opponent's discard.",
          intermediate: "Try to place the highest cards possible on your opponent's discard pile; being left with kings in your stock is bad. Remember the order of cards in your face-down stock to decide when to stop laying off onto the opponent and turn a stock card instead.",
          advanced: 'Balance giving cards to the opponent (to clear your pile) against giving them playable sequences; use knowledge of your stock order to time when to draw.',
          tips: [
            "Place high cards on the opponent's discard when you can.",
            'Remember the order of cards in your stock to plan when to turn instead of playing from discard.',
            'Kings left in stock are a disadvantage.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
